{{also|Leap|LEAP}}
==English==
{{wp}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈliːp/}}
** {{IPA|en|[ˈlɪi̯p]|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|En-us-leap.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈlɛp/|aa=obsolete,up to 19th c.|ref={{R:Jespersen MEGoHP|I|4.36|124}}}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːp|s=1}}
* {{hyph|en|leap}}

===Etymology 1===
{{etymid|en|jump}}
From {{inh|en|enm|lepen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|hlēapan}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*hlaupan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*hlaupaną}}. {{doublet|en|lope|lowp|elope|gallop|galop|interlope|loop}}.

Cognate with {{cog|frr|laap}}, {{m|frr|luup}}, {{m|frr|luupe||to jog, run, walk}}, {{cog|stq|lope}}, {{m|stq|loope||to run}}, {{cog|fy|ljeppe||to jump}}, {{cog|nl|lopen||to run; to walk}}, {{cog|de|laufen||to run; to walk}}, {{cog|li|loupe||to jog, run, walk}}, {{cog|nds|lopen}}, {{m|nds|loupen||to run}}, {{cog|lb|lafen||to run}}, {{cog|wym|łaojfa||to run}}, {{cog|da|løbe||to run}}, {{cog|fo|leypa||to jump}}, {{cog|is|hlaupa||to run; to jump}}, {{cog|nb|løpe||to run}}, {{cog|nn|laupa}}, {{m|nn|laupe}}, {{m|nn|løpa}}, {{m|nn|løpe||to run}}, {{cog|sv|löpa||to run}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*klewb-||to spring, stumble}} (compare {{cog|lt|šlùbti}} ‘to become lame’, {{m|lt|klùbti}} ‘to stumble’).

====Verb====
{{en-verb|+|+|+,~t,lept<l:archaic>,lope<l:obsolete>|+,~t,lept<l:obsolete>,lopen<l:obsolete>}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[jump]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1450|author=anonymous|title=Merlin
|passage=It is grete nede a man to go bak to recouer the better his '''leep'''}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1600|author=anonymous|title=The wisdome of Doctor Dodypoll|section=act 4
|passage=I, I defie thee: wert not thou next him when he '''leapt''' into the Riuer?}}
#* '''1783''', [[w:Hugh Blair|Hugh Blair]], from the “Iliad” in ''Lectures on Rhetoric and Belles Lettres'', lecture 4, page 65
#*: Th’ infernal monarch rear’d his horrid head, '''Leapt''' from his throne, lest Neptune’s arm should lay His dark dominions open to the day.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1920|author=Agatha Christie|authorlink=Agatha Christie|title={{w|The Mysterious Affair at Styles}}|publisher=Pan Books|location=London|year_published=1954|page=139|passage=“See you, one should not ask for outside proof—no, reason should be enough. But the flesh is weak, it is consolation to find that one is on the right track. Ah, my friend, I am like a giant refreshed. I run! I '''leap'''!” And, in very truth, run and '''leap''' he did, gambolling wildly down the stretch of lawn outside the long window.}}
#* '''1999''', Ai, ''Vice: New & Selected Poems'', page 78
#*: It is better to '''leap''' into the void.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To pass over by a leap or jump.
#: {{ux|en|to '''leap''' a wall or a ditch}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XL|62|⁠Deep folly! yet that this could be—<br>⁠That I could wing my will with might<br>⁠To '''leap''' the grades of life and light,<br>And flash at once, my friend, to thee: {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|transitive}} To [[copulate]] with (a female beast)
# {{lb|en|archaic|}} To [[copulate]] with (a human)
#* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Comedies and Tragedies|The Knight of Malta|passage=go '''leap''' her, and engender young devilings}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to leap.
#: {{ux|en|to '''leap''' a horse across a ditch}}

=====Usage notes=====
The choice between {{m|en|leapt}} and {{m|en|leaped}} is often generally a matter of regional differences: {{m|en|leapt}} is preferred in British English whereas {{m|en|leaped}} is somewhat more common in American English (although this is not to say that {{m|en|leapt}} is not used in American English, especially in areas with historical ties to England). According to research by John Algeo (''British or American English?'', Cambridge, 2006), {{m|en|leapt}} is used 80% of the time in UK and 32% in the US.

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|jump from one location to another}} {{l|en|bound}}, {{l|en|hop}}, {{l|en|jump}}, {{l|en|spring}}
* {{sense|jump upwards}} {{l|en|bound}}, {{l|en|hop}}, {{l|en|jump}}, {{l|en|spring}}

