====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# The act of [[leaping]] or [[jumping]].
#: {{ux|en|He made a '''leap''' across the river.}}
#* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables|passage=Leaps from one Extream to Another , are Unnatural Motions in the Course of our lives and Humours}}
#* '''1877''', Henry Sweet, ''A Handbook of Phonetics''{{attention|en|is this in the right section???}}
#*: Changes of tone may proceed either by '''leaps''' or glides.
# The [[distance]] traversed by a leap or [[jump]].
# {{lb|en|collective}} A group of [[leopards]].
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Calcutta Review|year=1970|page=373|url=https://books.google.com.sg/books?id=TronAQAAIAAJ|passage=Manikanta returned to the palace riding on a royal tiger accompanied by a '''leap''' of leopards to the utter surprise of the inhabitants of Pantalam.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Next Windows to be named "Vista".|date=2005-07-23|url=https://groups.google.com/forum/#!search/%22leap$20of$20leopards%22/comp.sys.mac.advocacy/Zlw_N7c0c4Y/FROaMIY-OykJ|passage=I can see it now... a '''leap''' of Leopards eating the carcass of a Longhorn out in the Vista....}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Cooper|title=The President's Dilemma: A Novel|year=2009|page=131|url=https://books.google.com.sg/books?isbn=1462830927|passage=Without the Chop Chop Chop Chop Cowville seems almost normal: no hover of helicopters, no '''leap''' of leopards.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Sandra Evans|title=This Is Not a Werewolf Story|year=2017|page=22|url=https://books.google.com.sg/books?isbn=1462830927|passage=I felt like the only one of my kind, and all around me were the other kids in their groups like herds of wildebeests and prides of lions and crashes of rhinos and unkindnesses of ravens and '''leaps''' of leopards and wrecks of sea hawks.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A [[significant]] [[move]] [[forward]].
#* {{quote-book|en|date=1969-07-20|author=[[w:Neil Armstrong|Neil Armstrong]]|title=as he became the first man to step on the moon|passage=That's one small step for [a] man, one giant '''leap''' for mankind.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=30-05-2024|title=How much online pornography is too much? A comparison of two theoretically distinct assessment scales|work=Archives of Public Health|volume=82|author=Germano Vera Cruz; Elias Aboujaoude;Magdalena Liberacka-Dwojak;Monika Wiłkość-Dębczyńska;Lucien Rochat; Riaz Khan; Yasser Khazaal|doi=10.1186/s13690-024-01294-5|passage=Since the arrival of mainstream pornography platforms in the early 2000’s and the rise in smartphone use, online pornography consumption has shown considerable year on year '''leaps'''.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A large step in [[reason]]ing, often one that is not justified by the facts.
#: {{ux|en|It's quite a '''leap''' to claim that those cloud formations are evidence of UFOs.}}
# {{lb|en|mining}} A [[fault]].
# [[copulation|Copulation]] (by a male animal) with a [[female]] one.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1865|title=British Farmer's Magazine|issue=48|page=8
|passage=Much difference of opinion exists as to the number of bullings a cow should receive. Here, I think, good judgment should be used. If the bull is cool and quiet, and some time has intervened since he had his last cow, one good '''leap''' is better than more {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|music}} A passing from one [[note]] to another by an [[interval]], especially by a long one, or by one including several other [[intermediate]] intervals.
# A small [[cataract]] over which fish attempt to jump; a [[salmon ladder]]<!--usually "salmon leap"-->.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1837|journal=The New Sporting Magazine|volume=12|page=358
|passage=He unfortunately persevered, and the cot veered round towards the fall of the '''leap''', and was running fast towards the rapids, when Mr. Craven lost his self-possession, and jumped out to gain a rock within a length of him, but did not succe[e]d, and he sunk in a part of the river over the '''leap''' called the dancing-hole, from which he was never more seen to rise. The cot was dashed with violence against another rock {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1868|author=John Marius Wilson|title=The Imperial Gazetteer of Scotland|page=24
|passage={{...}} where the Esk divides it in the middle, and forms a linn or '''leap''', is named the How burn; {{...}}}}

