#* {{quote-book|en|year=1994|title=The Magic, the Gathering: Pocket Players' Guide|author=|page=167|ISBN=1880992299|passage=At the conclusion of every duel, each player must show the remaining cards in his or her hand to the opponent to verify that no Restricted List duplicate cards appear there (e.g., to prove that a second Time Walk wasn't drawn from his '''library''').}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|title=Deckade: 10 Years of Decks, Thoughts, and Theory!|author=Michael J. Flores|page=228|ISBN=0977839508|passage=But why did very good players shy away from cards that are so obviously good from our perspectives? One very real possibility is that '''library''' manipulation/increased selection and redundancy tech had not yet been established - after all, many players were amazed at Lestree's use of the Sylvan Library to draw extra cards!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|title=Trading Card Games For Dummies|author=John Kaufeld; Jeremy Smith|page=50|ISBN=0470044071|passage=To win a game of Magic, you must achieve one of two chief goals: either be the first to reduce your opponent's life total from 20 to 0, or force him or her to attempt to draw a card from his or her '''library''' when there are no cards left in it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|title=Boy Culture: An Encyclopedia|author=Shirley R. Steinberg; Michael Kehler; Lindsay Cornish|page=418|ISBN=0313350817|passage=The deck becomes the draw pile, also called the '''library''', from which each player repeatedly draws cards for his or her own hand, cards that are gradually included into the game.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|title=Transactions on Edutainment III|author=Zhigeng Pan; Maiga Chang; Adrian David Cheok|page=119|ISBN=3642112447|passage=At this phase, most of TCGs only allow players drawing card(s) from a pack which is also called '''library''' or deck.}}

