===Etymology===
The {{lg|noun}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|leviathan,levyathan,levyethan}},{{ref|{{R:MED Online|entry=Leviathan|pos=n|id=MED25355}}}} from {{der|en|la-lat|leviathan}}, a {{lg|transliteration}} of {{der|en|hbo|לִוְיָתָן|tr=liwyāṯān}}, possibly from {{af|he|לִוְיָה<tr:liwyâ><t:garland, wreath>|-תָּן<tr:-tān><pos:{{lg|suffix}} forming {{lg|agent noun}}s>|lit=the tortuous one|nocat=1}}.

{{senseno|en|political state|uc=1}} (“political state”) was {{coinage|en|Q37621|nocap=1}} (1588–1679) in his work ''{{w|Leviathan (Hobbes)|Leviathan}}'' (1651): see the quotation. {{senseno|en|Satan|uc=1}} (“''synonym of'' Satan”) refers to [[w:Book of Isaiah|Isaiah]] 27:1 in the [[Bible]]{{ref|name=OED|{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=1053013004|date=March 2025|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}}} ([[King James Version]], spelling modernized): “In that day the Lord with his sore and great and strong sword shall punish Leviathan the piercing serpent, even Leviathan that crooked serpent, and he shall slay the dragon that is in the sea.”<ref>{{R:King James Version|Isaiah|27|1|page=782|column=2|passage=In that day the {{smallcaps|Lord}} with his ſore and great and ſtrong ſworde ſhall puniſh Leuiathan the piercing ſerpent, even Leuiathan that crooked ſerpent, and hee ſhall ſlay the dragon that ''is'' in the Sea.}}</ref>

The {{lg|adjective}} is from an {{lg|attributive}} use of the noun.

