##* {{RQ:Dekker Newes from Hell|sig=G3|page=50|passage=Of this laſt requeſt, the Lacquy of this great '''Leuiathan''', promiſde he ſhould be maiſter, but he vvould not bring him to a miles end by land, (they vvere too many to meddle vvith).}}
##* {{RQ:Sanderson Sermons|edition=1656|para=36|page=412|passage=So can the [[w:Jesus|Lord]] deal, and often doth, vvith the great ''Behemoths'' and '''''Leviathans''''' of the vvorld: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Burke Noble Lord|page=37|passage=The Duke of Bedford {{quote-gloss|{{w|Francis Russell, 5th Duke of Bedford}}}} is the '''Leviathan''' among all the creatures of the Crovvn. He tumbles about his unvvieldy bulk; he plays and frolicks in the ocean of the Royal bounty.}}
##* {{RQ:Byron Childe Harold|canto=IV|stanza=CLXXXI|page=93|passage=The oak '''leviathans''', whose huge ribs make / Their clay creator the vain title take / Of lord of thee, and arbiter of war; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:De Quincey Works|volume=II|page=263|passage=[F]oreseeing the result of a legal contest with so potent a defendant as this '''leviathan''' of two counties, and that, under any nominal award, the whole estate of the orphans might be swallowed up on the costs of any suit that should be carried into Chancery, they prudently withdrew from all active measures of opposition, confiding the event to Lord Lonsdale {{quote-gloss|{{w|William Lowther, 1st Earl of Lonsdale}}}}'s returning sense of justice.}}
##* {{RQ:Dickens Old Curiosity Shop|chapter=27|page=242|passage=When she had exhibited these '''leviathans''' of public announcement {{quote-gloss|large canvas scrolls}} to the astonished child, she brought forth specimens of the lesser fry in the shape of hand-bills, some of which were couched in the form of parodies on popular melodies, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Trollope Warden|chapter=The Warden's Decision|page=200|passage=It {{quote-gloss|a newspaper article}} named some sons of bishops, and grandsons of archbishops; men great in their way, who had redeemed their disgrace in the eyes of many by the enormity of their plunder; and then, having disposed of these '''leviathans''', it descended to Mr Harding.}}
##* {{RQ:Wells War of the Worlds|page=183|passage=Keeping his footing on the heaving deck by clutching the bulwarks, my brother looked past this charging '''leviathan''' {{quote-gloss|a [[torpedo ram]] warship}} at the Martians again, and he saw the three of them now close together, and standing so far out to sea that their tripod supports were almost entirely submerged.}}
##* {{RQ:Wired|date=7 October 2020|author=[[w:Cecilia D'Anastasio|Cecilia D’Anastasio]]|title=Amazon Wants to ‘Win at Games.’ So Why Hasn’t It?|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250725182327/https://www.wired.com/story/amazon-wants-to-win-at-games-so-why-hasnt-it/|passage=After brute-forcing its way to dominance in so many industries, the tech '''leviathan''' may finally have met its match {{quote-gloss|subtitle}}.}}
## {{senseid|en|political state}}{{lb|en|political science}} ''Sometimes in the form'' '''[[Leviathan]]''': [[base#Verb|based]] on the [[writing#Noun|writings]] of {{w|Thomas Hobbes}}, the [[political#Adjective|political]] [[state#Noun|state]], especially a [[domineering#Adjective|domineering]] and [[totalitarian#Adjective|totalitarian]] one.
##: {{hol|en|system<id:the system, typically run by the Man><alt:the System>}}
##: {{cot|en|Big Brother#Proper noun|the man#Noun<alt:the Man>}}
##* {{RQ:Hobbes Leviathan|chapter=Of the Causes, Generation, and Definition of a Common-wealth|page=87|passage=[T]he Multitude ſo united in one Perſon, is called a {{smallcaps|Common-vvealth}}, in latine {{smallcaps|Civitas}}. This is the generation of that great '''{{smallcaps|Leviathan}}''', or rather (to ſpeake more reverently) of that ''Mortall God'', to vvhich vvee ovve under the ''Immortall [[w:God in Christianity|God]]'', our peace and defence.}}
##* {{RQ:Locke Human Understanding|chapter=No Innate Practical Principles|section=5|page=17|passage=[I]f a Chriſtian, vvho has the view of Happineſs and Miſery in another Life, be asked vvhy a Man muſt keep his VVord, he vvill ''give'' this as ''a Reaſon'': Becauſe [[w:God in Christianity|God]], vvho has the Povver of eternal Life and Death, requires it of us. But if an ''Hobbiſt'' {{quote-gloss|a follower of the doctrines of {{w|Thomas Hobbes}}}} be asked vvhy; he vvill anſvver, Becauſe the Publick requires it, and the '''''Leviathan''''' vvill puniſh you, if you do not.}}
##* {{RQ:Arendt Totalitarianism|edition=new|page=140|passage=The fact is that [[w:Thomas Hobbes|{{quote-gloss|Thomas}} Hobbes]] is interested in neither, but concerned exclusively with the political structure itself, and he depicts the features of man according to the needs of the '''Leviathan'''.}}
## {{senseid|en|Satan}}{{lb|en|obsolete}} {{synonym of|en|Satan|t=the [[supreme#Adjective|supreme]] [[evil#Adjective|evil]] [[spirit#Noun|spirit]] in the [[Abrahamic#Adjective|Abrahamic]] [[religion]]s, who [[tempt]]s [[humanity]] into [[sin#Noun|sin]]; the [[Devil]]}}.
##* {{RQ:Barnes Spirituall Sonnets|sonnet=XLVIII|sig=&#91;D4&#93;|page=30|passage=[S]eu'n times thrice more glorious the name, / By vvhich thrice povverfull vvee coniure the ſame: / VVhich but repeated doth that Dragon feare, / That olde '''Leuyathan''' vvhoſe iavves Lord teare. / {{...}} / Then glorious Captaine, our chiefe God and man, / Breake thou the Iavves of old '''Leuiathan'''.}}

