===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[stay]] or [[remain]] in a place or situation, especially as if [[unwilling]] to depart or not easily able to do so.
#: {{syn|en|abide|loiter|tarry|Thesaurus:tarry}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|act=II|scene=ix|page=172|passage=Still more foole I ſhall appeare / By the time I '''linger''' here}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1859|author=w:Charles Dickens|title=A Tale of Two Cities|chapter=15|passage=His tone '''lingered''' in the air, almost like the tone of a musical instrument.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1891|work=Scribner’s Magazine|month=July|author=w:Edith Wharton|title=Mrs. Manstey's View|passage=She '''lingered''' in the window until the windy sunset died in bat-colored dusk; then, going to bed, she lay sleepless all night.|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/295/295-h/295-h.htm#link2H_4_0005}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1931|title=w:Dream a Little Dream of Me|lyricist=w:Gus Kahn|composer=Fabian Andre; Wilbur Schwandt|passage=Stars fading but I '''linger''' on, dear / Still craving your kiss / I'm longin' to '''linger''' till dawn, dear / Just saying this}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2011 April 25|author=Alice Park|url=http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,2065236,00.html|title=Upgrading the Disaster|work=Time|passage=It takes into account {{...}} predictions of how long radioactive contaminants will '''linger''' in the soil and water near the nuclear facility.|accessdate=19 September 2011|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20111203140025/http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,2065236,00.html|archivedate=3 December 2011}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=2016 January 30|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/opinion/sunday/hillary-clinton-endorsement.html?smid=fb-nytopinion&smtyp=cur&_r=0|title=Hillary Clinton for the Democratic Nomination|work=The New York Times|accessdate=30 January 2016|passage=Mrs. Clinton’s main opponent, Senator Bernie Sanders, a self-described Democratic Socialist, has proved to be more formidable than most people, including Mrs. Clinton, anticipated. He has brought income inequality and the '''lingering''' pain of the middle class to center stage and pushed Mrs. Clinton a bit more to the left than she might have gone on economic issues.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20251104210402/https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/opinion/sunday/hillary-clinton-endorsement.html?smid=fb-nytopinion&smtyp=cur&_r=0|archivedate=4 November 2025}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To remain [[alive]] or [[existent]] although still proceeding toward [[death]] or [[extinction]]; to die [[gradually]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1887|author=w:Thomas Hardy|title=The Woodlanders|chapter=14|passage=He '''lingered''' through the day, and died that evening as the sun went down.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1904|author=w:Andrew Lang|chapter=Asmund and Signy|title=The Brown Fairy Book|passage=During his absence the queen fell ill, and after '''lingering''' for some time she died.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|idiomatic|often followed by {{m|en|on}}}} To [[consider]] or [[contemplate]] for a period of time; to engage in [[analytic]] thinking or [[discussion]].
#* {{RQ:Time|date=14 April 2011|author=[[w:Michael Scherer (journalist)|Michael Scherer]]|title=Trump's Political Reality Show: Will the Donald Really Run for President?|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20110923140506/http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,2065235,00.html|passage=Trump doesn't '''linger''' on the poll.}}

